1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,510
October 1952. Cheorwon, Gangwon Province.

2
00:00:04,637 --> 00:00:07,952
On a nameless hill just 395 meters high,

3
00:00:07,972 --> 00:00:10,290
two armies staked everything.

4
00:00:10,512 --> 00:00:12,759
The Chinese command's math was simple.

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,345
Three times the men.

6
00:00:14,365 --> 00:00:17,293
Push hard, and the hill falls.

7
00:00:17,523 --> 00:00:21,243
In ten days, about 280,000 shells from

8
00:00:21,263 --> 00:00:24,372
both sides fell on this one hill.

9
00:00:24,443 --> 00:00:25,971
The forest burned away, and the

10
00:00:25,991 --> 00:00:28,461
ridgeline was stripped bare and white.

11
00:00:28,688 --> 00:00:30,543
In that time, the hill is said to

12
00:00:30,563 --> 00:00:33,206
have changed hands twenty-four times.

13
00:00:33,452 --> 00:00:37,229
On one side, the ROK 9th Infantry Division.

14
00:00:37,249 --> 00:00:40,966
On the other, the elite Chinese 38th Army.

15
00:00:41,016 --> 00:00:42,225
The attackers outnumbered the

16
00:00:42,245 --> 00:00:44,465
defenders more than three to one.

17
00:00:44,845 --> 00:00:46,088
And ten days later,

18
00:00:46,108 --> 00:00:47,465
the men still standing on

19
00:00:47,485 --> 00:00:49,882
that hill were South Korean.

20
00:00:50,106 --> 00:00:51,701
When the smoke cleared, the hill looked

21
00:00:51,721 --> 00:00:54,170
like a white horse lying on its side.

22
00:00:54,276 --> 00:00:55,701
This is the battle that decided

23
00:00:55,721 --> 00:00:57,658
the fate of the Cheorwon plain:

24
00:00:57,678 --> 00:01:00,314
the Battle of White Horse Hill.

25
00:01:00,540 --> 00:01:02,550
By 1952, the war was

26
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:04,440
entering its third year.

27
00:01:04,517 --> 00:01:07,569
The front had stalled near the 38th Parallel,

28
00:01:07,589 --> 00:01:10,644
as truce talks dragged on at Panmunjom.

29
00:01:10,894 --> 00:01:12,776
Where the ceasefire line would be drawn

30
00:01:12,796 --> 00:01:15,746
depended on who held which ground.

31
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:17,833
So both sides poured blood

32
00:01:17,853 --> 00:01:19,678
into every single hill.

33
00:01:19,917 --> 00:01:22,863
At the center lay the so-called Iron Triangle,

34
00:01:22,883 --> 00:01:26,447
linking Cheorwon, Kimhwa, and Pyonggang.

35
00:01:26,553 --> 00:01:28,002
The heart of the central front,

36
00:01:28,022 --> 00:01:29,995
where every major road met.

37
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:32,759
And northwest of the Cheorwon plain,

38
00:01:32,779 --> 00:01:36,662
a lone ridge looked down over the flatland.

39
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:37,995
On the maps, it carried

40
00:01:38,015 --> 00:01:41,153
only a number: Hill 395.

41
00:01:41,384 --> 00:01:43,001
The plain around it sat at roughly

42
00:01:43,021 --> 00:01:45,072
200 meters above sea level.

43
00:01:45,182 --> 00:01:46,596
From this solitary peak,

44
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:49,942
the entire Cheorwon area lay open below.

45
00:01:50,172 --> 00:01:52,511
If it fell, the U.S. IX Corps would

46
00:01:52,531 --> 00:01:54,948
have to give up the Cheorwon area.

47
00:01:54,968 --> 00:01:56,713
For the Chinese, it would

48
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:59,031
open a road leading south.

49
00:01:59,273 --> 00:02:01,522
The hill was entrusted to Major General Kim

50
00:02:01,542 --> 00:02:05,504
Jong-oh, commander of the ROK 9th Division.

51
00:02:05,571 --> 00:02:07,884
On the first day of the war, he had held the

52
00:02:07,904 --> 00:02:10,881
North Koreans at Chuncheon for three days.

53
00:02:11,117 --> 00:02:14,196
In 1951, the 9th Division had been rebuilt

54
00:02:14,216 --> 00:02:16,939
through a grueling retraining program.

55
00:02:17,025 --> 00:02:19,168
Now, about 12,000 of its

56
00:02:19,188 --> 00:02:21,187
men faced their true test.

57
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:24,599
Their opponent was the Chinese 38th Army.

58
00:02:24,723 --> 00:02:27,388
In the winter of 1950, Peng Dehuai

59
00:02:27,408 --> 00:02:28,960
was said to have hailed it with the

60
00:02:28,980 --> 00:02:32,485
words 'Long live the 38th Army.'

61
00:02:32,715 --> 00:02:34,796
On October 3rd, a Chinese officer

62
00:02:34,816 --> 00:02:37,162
crossed over to the South Korean lines.

63
00:02:37,251 --> 00:02:40,088
What he revealed was alarming: a full-scale

64
00:02:40,108 --> 00:02:42,635
assault on this hill was coming soon.

65
00:02:42,887 --> 00:02:45,517
The 9th Division ringed the hill with tanks,

66
00:02:45,537 --> 00:02:48,316
anti-aircraft guns, and searchlights.

67
00:02:48,485 --> 00:02:49,934
Flares to turn night into

68
00:02:49,954 --> 00:02:51,827
day were piled high.

69
00:02:52,066 --> 00:02:54,594
October 6th, 1952.

70
00:02:54,649 --> 00:02:58,636
Chinese shells began to blanket Hill 395.

71
00:02:58,895 --> 00:03:00,405
At the same time, the Chinese

72
00:03:00,425 --> 00:03:01,873
blew open the floodgates of the

73
00:03:01,893 --> 00:03:04,080
Pongnae reservoir to the north.

74
00:03:04,179 --> 00:03:05,844
The swollen Yokkok Stream cut

75
00:03:05,864 --> 00:03:07,779
off the hill's rear, so ROK

76
00:03:07,799 --> 00:03:09,888
reinforcements could not cross.

77
00:03:10,112 --> 00:03:13,354
As night fell, Chinese infantry swarmed the

78
00:03:13,374 --> 00:03:16,107
slopes to the sound of bugles and whistles.

79
00:03:16,276 --> 00:03:17,690
When the front rank fell,

80
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:20,431
the next rank climbed right over them.

81
00:03:20,649 --> 00:03:22,712
South Korean and American artillery

82
00:03:22,732 --> 00:03:24,101
laid a barrage in front of

83
00:03:24,121 --> 00:03:26,251
the hill with eighty-one guns.

84
00:03:26,361 --> 00:03:28,743
On the first night alone, an estimated

85
00:03:28,763 --> 00:03:31,388
five hundred Chinese soldiers fell.

86
00:03:31,631 --> 00:03:33,641
Around the same time, Chinese

87
00:03:33,661 --> 00:03:36,601
troops also massed against Hill 281,

88
00:03:36,621 --> 00:03:39,059
'Arrowhead,' to the southeast.

89
00:03:39,169 --> 00:03:40,584
The French Battalion holding

90
00:03:40,604 --> 00:03:43,055
it threw back every assault.

91
00:03:43,292 --> 00:03:44,756
Control of the hill flipped

92
00:03:44,776 --> 00:03:46,361
between night and day.

93
00:03:46,381 --> 00:03:48,296
By night the Chinese took it; by

94
00:03:48,316 --> 00:03:50,718
morning the South Koreans took it back.

95
00:03:50,957 --> 00:03:52,468
When ammunition ran out in the

96
00:03:52,488 --> 00:03:54,722
trenches, men fought hand to hand

97
00:03:54,742 --> 00:03:56,941
with bayonets and rifle butts.

98
00:03:57,126 --> 00:03:58,665
Day by day, the soil on the

99
00:03:58,685 --> 00:04:00,881
hill turned the color of blood.

100
00:04:01,102 --> 00:04:02,966
On October 10th, at 4:30 in the

101
00:04:02,986 --> 00:04:05,333
morning, the hill was lost again.

102
00:04:05,410 --> 00:04:07,496
But at 8:15 that same morning,

103
00:04:07,516 --> 00:04:10,365
South Korean troops took it back once more.

104
00:04:10,595 --> 00:04:12,615
At the Hwarang Hill outpost,

105
00:04:12,635 --> 00:04:15,153
23-year-old platoon leader 2nd Lt.

106
00:04:15,173 --> 00:04:18,557
Lee Seong-deok fought on, surrounded.

107
00:04:18,694 --> 00:04:20,699
Out of ammunition and water, he held to

108
00:04:20,719 --> 00:04:24,009
the end, and was killed on October 7th.

109
00:04:24,242 --> 00:04:26,111
General Kim rotated his regiments

110
00:04:26,131 --> 00:04:28,399
into the fight, one after another.

111
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:30,379
When the 28th Regiment was spent,

112
00:04:30,399 --> 00:04:33,823
in went the 29th, and then the 30th.

113
00:04:34,048 --> 00:04:36,181
Most counterattacks came at night.

114
00:04:36,318 --> 00:04:38,050
Under the glare of flares,

115
00:04:38,070 --> 00:04:39,951
small platoons crawled up the

116
00:04:39,971 --> 00:04:42,321
ridgeline with grenades in hand.

117
00:04:42,548 --> 00:04:44,740
At first light, American fighter-bombers

118
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:47,049
came screaming down from the sky.

119
00:04:47,157 --> 00:04:50,290
More than 358 napalm bombs alone

120
00:04:50,310 --> 00:04:52,386
fell on and around the hill.

121
00:04:52,766 --> 00:04:54,103
October 12th.

122
00:04:54,124 --> 00:04:56,198
The 1st Battalion, 30th Regiment,

123
00:04:56,218 --> 00:04:58,087
moved out against the main peak,

124
00:04:58,107 --> 00:05:00,561
once again in Chinese hands.

125
00:05:00,796 --> 00:05:03,168
First, U.S. artillery fired a

126
00:05:03,188 --> 00:05:05,205
'Time on Target' strike,

127
00:05:05,225 --> 00:05:07,845
many batteries hitting one point at once.

128
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:09,964
Two Chinese battalions forming up to

129
00:05:09,984 --> 00:05:12,473
attack were wiped out where they stood.

130
00:05:12,692 --> 00:05:15,290
But just below the summit, a single Chinese

131
00:05:15,310 --> 00:05:17,872
machine-gun bunker blocked the way.

132
00:05:17,951 --> 00:05:20,014
Again and again, 1st Company's

133
00:05:20,034 --> 00:05:21,719
charges broke against it.

134
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:23,900
Then platoon leader 2nd Lt.

135
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:27,599
Kang Seung-woo and two soldiers, Oh Gyu-bong

136
00:05:27,619 --> 00:05:30,546
and Ahn Young-kwon, stepped forward.

137
00:05:30,772 --> 00:05:32,811
Clutching explosives and grenades,

138
00:05:32,831 --> 00:05:36,077
they threw themselves at the machine-gun nest.

139
00:05:36,477 --> 00:05:39,072
The bunker collapsed in a thunderous blast,

140
00:05:39,092 --> 00:05:41,307
and the three men never came back.

141
00:05:41,556 --> 00:05:42,362
In that opening,

142
00:05:42,382 --> 00:05:44,877
1st Company charged all at once.

143
00:05:44,932 --> 00:05:47,257
At 1:20 p.m., the main peak of Hill

144
00:05:47,277 --> 00:05:50,709
395 was back in South Korean hands.

145
00:05:50,944 --> 00:05:52,808
In later years, they would be remembered

146
00:05:52,828 --> 00:05:55,777
as the 'Three Heroes of White Horse Hill.'

147
00:05:55,996 --> 00:05:58,657
What remained was Camel Ridge,

148
00:05:58,677 --> 00:06:00,532
northwest of the peak.

149
00:06:00,611 --> 00:06:03,437
On the morning of October 14th, the 29th

150
00:06:03,457 --> 00:06:06,160
Regiment finally seized that ridge as well.

151
00:06:06,403 --> 00:06:07,914
That night, the Chinese 38th

152
00:06:07,934 --> 00:06:09,679
Army finally pulled back.

153
00:06:09,749 --> 00:06:11,208
Ten days of fighting for the

154
00:06:11,228 --> 00:06:13,473
hill had come to an end.

155
00:06:13,702 --> 00:06:15,241
As the smoke lifted, people were

156
00:06:15,261 --> 00:06:17,585
stunned by what was left of the hill.

157
00:06:17,708 --> 00:06:20,158
The forest was gone, and the stripped ridge

158
00:06:20,178 --> 00:06:22,980
looked like a white horse lying down.

159
00:06:23,221 --> 00:06:24,597
And so it got its name:

160
00:06:24,617 --> 00:06:27,830
'Baengma-goji,' White Horse Hill.

161
00:06:28,064 --> 00:06:30,439
Against an enemy three times its size,

162
00:06:30,459 --> 00:06:33,330
how did the ROK Army hold this hill?

163
00:06:33,406 --> 00:06:35,606
Let's break it down into four factors.

164
00:06:35,832 --> 00:06:38,420
First, they knew the attack was coming.

165
00:06:38,664 --> 00:06:40,782
Thanks to the defector's warning, the 9th

166
00:06:40,802 --> 00:06:43,832
Division had three days to fortify the hill.

167
00:06:43,861 --> 00:06:45,560
What might have been a surprise attack

168
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:48,145
walked straight into a prepared trap.

169
00:06:48,366 --> 00:06:50,519
Second, firepower.

170
00:06:50,753 --> 00:06:53,078
South Korean and UN guns fired

171
00:06:53,098 --> 00:06:56,014
more than 185,000 rounds.

172
00:06:56,069 --> 00:06:58,801
The Chinese fired about 55,000.

173
00:06:59,019 --> 00:07:00,451
Third, a commander's instinct

174
00:07:00,471 --> 00:07:02,648
for handling his reserves.

175
00:07:02,869 --> 00:07:04,875
General Kim sent in a fresh regiment

176
00:07:04,895 --> 00:07:07,481
before the one in the line could collapse.

177
00:07:07,581 --> 00:07:08,938
He never gave the Chinese a

178
00:07:08,958 --> 00:07:10,982
moment to catch their breath.

179
00:07:11,212 --> 00:07:13,332
Fourth, a South Korean army

180
00:07:13,352 --> 00:07:15,024
that had remade itself.

181
00:07:15,246 --> 00:07:17,891
Barely a year earlier, ROK units had

182
00:07:17,911 --> 00:07:20,611
often crumbled before Chinese offensives.

183
00:07:20,734 --> 00:07:23,867
Now, after intense retraining, that same

184
00:07:23,887 --> 00:07:27,241
army stopped the elite 38th Army head-on.

185
00:07:27,481 --> 00:07:29,226
According to South Korean records,

186
00:07:29,246 --> 00:07:33,367
the 9th Division suffered 3,422 casualties.

187
00:07:33,523 --> 00:07:35,256
Chinese casualties are put at

188
00:07:35,276 --> 00:07:37,738
over 14,000, though Chinese

189
00:07:37,758 --> 00:07:40,494
sources give a figure of 6,700.

190
00:07:40,736 --> 00:07:42,446
Behind every one of those numbers was

191
00:07:42,466 --> 00:07:44,942
someone's son, someone's brother.

192
00:07:45,187 --> 00:07:48,063
After this battle, the ROK 9th Division earned

193
00:07:48,083 --> 00:07:51,209
a new name: the 'White Horse Division.'

194
00:07:51,306 --> 00:07:53,039
That name remains the symbol

195
00:07:53,059 --> 00:07:54,816
of the division to this day.

196
00:07:55,046 --> 00:07:58,130
Then, on July 27th, 1953,

197
00:07:58,150 --> 00:08:00,508
the Armistice Agreement was signed.

198
00:08:00,684 --> 00:08:03,385
White Horse Hill was left just as it was,

199
00:08:03,405 --> 00:08:06,933
inside the Demilitarized Zone.

200
00:08:07,177 --> 00:08:08,864
South of the hill, a battlefield

201
00:08:08,884 --> 00:08:10,947
memorial and monument now honor

202
00:08:10,967 --> 00:08:13,107
the sacrifices of those days.

203
00:08:13,352 --> 00:08:15,665
Even today, the remains of the war dead

204
00:08:15,685 --> 00:08:18,237
are still being recovered in this area.

205
00:08:18,483 --> 00:08:21,189
A hill just 395 meters high.

206
00:08:21,295 --> 00:08:22,914
Beneath that number, thousands

207
00:08:22,934 --> 00:08:24,966
of young lives lie at rest.

208
00:08:25,357 --> 00:08:27,865
A mountain stripped as white as a horse.

209
00:08:27,941 --> 00:08:29,936
And those who held it to the very end

210
00:08:29,956 --> 00:08:32,523
were the ones who would not step back.

211
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:34,341
If this story meant something to you,

212
00:08:34,361 --> 00:08:36,640
please subscribe and like the video.

213
00:08:36,694 --> 00:08:37,711
We'll continue to bring the

214
00:08:37,731 --> 00:08:39,650
forgotten records of war to light.
